    "content": "# Block Ops Vision\n\n## Overview\n\nBlock Ops\u2019 vision is to become the standard infrastructure layer for site-level regional anesthesia programs.\n\nThe long-term aim is not just to help one facility perform nerve blocks better. It is to make high-quality nerve-block delivery more repeatable across sites by standardizing the system around the work.\n\n## Working vision statement\n\n> Block Ops aims to become the standard system surgical facilities use to build, support, and sustain reliable nerve-block programs.\n\n## Plain-English version\n\nThe future Block Ops is building toward:\n\n> A facility should not need to invent its nerve-block program from scratch.\n\nInstead, Block Ops gives the facility a proven structure: the workflows, roles, materials, training, documentation, digital references, and program intelligence needed to make regional anesthesia more reliable at the site level.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nNerve blocks can create major value for a surgical facility, but only when the surrounding system is strong enough to support them consistently.\n\nThe vision is for Block Ops to make that system portable:\n\n- a repeatable foundation every site starts from\n- a site-specific layer that adapts to local formulary, staffing, workflow, equipment, EMR, and surgical mix\n- a client-facing experience that is simple, approved, and usable\n- data capture over time so the program becomes more measurable and improvable\n\n## What success looks like\n\nBlock Ops is successful if a facility can say:\n\n- We know where and how blocks fit into our workflow.\n- Our nursing and provider roles are clear.\n- Our materials, carts, and references are standardized.\n- Our documentation is easier to complete consistently.\n- Our team has training and competency support.\n- Our patients receive clear education and safety guidance.\n- Our program does not depend on one person remembering everything.\n- We can track the program well enough to improve it over time.\n\n## Strategic direction\n\nBlock Ops should compound into three durable assets:\n\n1. **Implementation system** \u2014 a repeatable way to assess, build, launch, and support nerve-block programs.\n2. **Knowledge and deliverables library** \u2014 the protocols, templates, checklists, training materials, patient materials, and implementation bundles that make the system usable.\n3. **Digital platform and data layer** \u2014 the Dashboard, Mission Control, client portal, reference tools, tracking, and eventual outcome/value intelligence that make Block Ops scalable and provable.\n\n## Current standard / process\n\nThe vision should guide major Block Ops decisions:\n\n- Does this make nerve-block delivery more repeatable at the site level?\n- Does this strengthen the infrastructure around blocks?\n- Does this preserve the boundary that Block Ops supports and educates but does not provide clinical care?\n- Does this make the system easier to configure for a real facility?\n- Does this create durable knowledge, workflow, data, or implementation value?\n\n## What the vision does not claim yet\n\nThis vision does not claim that Block Ops has already proven guaranteed outcome, revenue, opioid, discharge, throughput, or ROI improvements.\n\nThose claims require separate evidence and credibility review before becoming approved language.\n\n## Related Mission Control workflows/tasks\n\n- Wiki migration / Foundation identity batch\n- Mission and positioning review\n- Claim review / evidence validation\n- Digital platform buildout\n- Implementation bundle review\n\n## Related Wiki pages\n\n- [[Block Ops Mission]]\n- [[Block Ops Positioning and Core Story]]\n- [[Block Ops North Star]]\n- [[Block Ops System Architecture]]\n- [[Pillar Model - Gold Standard Tree]]\n- [[Implementation Bundles]]\n- [[Block Ops Wiki Classification Rules]]\n\n## Open questions / needs update\n\n1. Does \u201cstandard infrastructure layer\u201d feel too technical, or does it capture the long-term vision?\n2. Should the vision lean more toward patient impact, facility operations, company value, or platform/data moat?\n3. Is \u201creliable nerve-block programs\u201d the right plain-English phrase?\n4. How much of the future platform/data moat should appear in the core vision versus internal strategy notes?\n",
    "kind": "wiki-page"
