    "content": "# Implementation Bundles\n\n## Overview\n\nImplementation Bundles are the productized materials that make the Block Ops system usable at a real site.\n\nThey are the concrete protocols, checklists, templates, patient materials, training tools, documentation aids, safety references, and workflow resources that turn the Block Ops operating model into something a facility can actually use.\n\nSimple definition:\n\n> Implementation Bundles are the practical materials a site receives to help build, run, and sustain its nerve-block infrastructure.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nBlock Ops is not only advice or a one-time training visit.\n\nThe value is that the facility receives a structured system it can keep using after implementation:\n\n- staff can reference the same standards\n- nurses know their role\n- patients get consistent education\n- carts and supplies are easier to maintain\n- documentation is easier to standardize\n- safety processes are visible\n- the program has reusable operating memory\n\nImplementation Bundles are how the system becomes tangible.\n\n## How it works\n\nImplementation Bundles should be organized into layers:\n\n1. **Foundation bundle** \u2014 the universal baseline every site needs before specialty customization.\n2. **Block-specific bundles** \u2014 future procedure/block-family packs that adapt the system to surgical mix.\n3. **Site-specific adaptations** \u2014 local formulary, equipment, EMR, staffing, workflow, and leadership requirements.\n4. **Client-facing approved outputs** \u2014 only final, reviewed materials that are safe for client use.\n\n## Foundation bundle\n\nThe Foundation bundle is the universal baseline.\n\nIt should contain the core materials needed to make a nerve-block program operational before adding block-specific pathways.\n\nCurrent source folders include draft materials across:\n\n### Physical Operations\n\n- block cart par-level guide\n- block cart planogram\n- block-bay workflow logic\n- daily cart restock checklist\n- machine cleaning checklist\n- probe cover and gel management standard\n- clean vs sterile protocol\n\n### Human Capital / Training\n\n- block lead nurse responsibility list\n- block champion charter\n- LAST second responder nursing competency\n- sterile setup assist competency\n- sedation administration / monitoring SOP\n\n### Patient / Stakeholder Integration\n\n- nerve block patient brochure\n- post-block sensory guide\n- patient red flag card\n- fall prevention patient agreement\n- red flag recognition card\n- breakthrough pain protocol\n- fall risk assessment\n\n### Clinical Safety / Protocol Reference\n\n- RA consent form\n- LAST protocol suite\n- high-volume dilution chart\n- weight-based max dose calculator\n- standardized test dose protocol\n- block time-out checklist\n- LA selection algorithm\n- adjuvant dosing guide\n- intraneural injection stop criteria\n- hydrodissection / opening pressure protocol\n- Exparel utilization criteria\n\n### Documentation / EMR / Value Intelligence\n\n- block procedure note template\n- PACU block assessment smart template\n- pre-op nursing smart template\n- block success / failure log\n- block status tracking board guide\n- MME calculator reference\n- PACU LOS tracker\n- medical necessity phrases\n- CPT / ICD-10 crosswalk\n\n### Ultrasound / Technique\n\n- in-plane technique SOP\n- out-of-plane technique SOP\n- triangle of success setup guide\n- PART maneuver guide\n- knobology cheat sheet\n\n## Current standard / process\n\nImplementation Bundles should be treated carefully because they are close to the core product.\n\nCurrent rules:\n\n1. Deliverables marked final and reviewed by Samir can be treated as verified final content.\n2. Deliverables not marked final are drafts.\n3. Draft deliverables can support internal M1 mock-client testing, but they should not be treated as verified, final, or client-facing.\n4. Clients should only see approved final deliverables.\n5. Legal, reimbursement, billing, clinical-outcome, and compliance-sensitive materials require detailed review before promotion.\n6. Source files should be preserved during migration; do not bulk-copy all draft deliverables into the live Wiki.\n\n## M1 handling\n\nFor M1, the goal is not to prove every deliverable is final.\n\nThe goal is to test whether a mock client can move through the lead-to-live workflow and receive the right kind of materials in the right order.\n\nM1 can use draft deliverables if they are clearly labeled and the workflow can track their status:\n\n- draft\n- current\n- needs review\n- final / approved\n- missing\n\n## Review standard\n\nBefore an Implementation Bundle item becomes source-of-truth or client-facing, Samir needs to review it for accuracy and completeness.\n\nFor legal, compliance, reimbursement, or liability-sensitive items, counsel or qualified review may also be required.\n\n## Related Mission Control workflows/tasks\n\n- Wiki migration / Foundation identity batch\n- Foundation deliverable inventory\n- M1 mock-client lead-to-live run\n- M2 foundational-client readiness\n- Legal/compliance review\n- Claim review / evidence validation\n- Client portal content approval\n\n## Related Wiki pages\n\n- [[Pillar Model - Gold Standard Tree]]\n- [[Block Ops Mission]]\n- [[Block Ops Positioning and Core Story]]\n- [[Block Ops Vision]]\n- [[Block Ops Wiki Classification Rules]]\n- [[Block Ops Wiki Master Content Inventory]]\n\n## Open questions / needs update\n\n1. Should the sixth pillar keep the name \u201cImplementation Bundles,\u201d or should it later be renamed to something more intuitive?\n2. Which Foundation deliverables are already final versus draft?\n3. Which deliverables are required for the M1 mock-client run?\n4. Which deliverables must be reviewed before any real foundational client sees them?\n5. How should block-specific bundles be named and packaged later?\n6. Which deliverables belong in the internal Wiki versus the client portal versus source archive only?\n",
